&lt;h1&gt;OHA urges vaccinations as flu activity rises, holidays approach&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PORTLAND, Ore &ndash; As influenza activity increases over the next two months before its expected peak in early spring, health officials urge everyone to get their flu vaccine, particularly as people gather indoors to celebrate the holidays.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So far, hospitalizations remain low. But the percentage of positive flu tests is on the rise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;The flu season generally continues into the spring, which means flu activity will continue to increase for many months,&rdquo; said Howard Chiou, M.D., Ph.D., medical director for communicable diseases and immunizations at Oregon Health Authority&rsquo;s Public Health Division. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s why it&rsquo;s never too late to get the flu vaccine.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chiou added that getting vaccinated now is especially important. &ldquo;People will leave Thanksgiving, not only with leftovers, but also with the flu. It&rsquo;s not too late to get your flu shot, especially before the upcoming holidays. The flu shot is the best way to help make sure you don&rsquo;t miss out on holiday festivities or get other people sick.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last year&rsquo;s flu season in Oregon was the worst in the state&rsquo;s recent history. OHA releases the latest information on the flu season in its weekly &lt;a href="https://links-2.govdelivery.com/CL0/https:%2F%2Fwww.oregon.gov%2Foha%2Fph%2Fdiseasesconditions%2Fcommunicabledisease%2Fdiseasesurveillancedata%2Finfluenza%2Fpages%2Fsurveil.aspx%3Futm_medium=email%26utm_source=govdelivery/1/0101019ae6cc9a96-bbf9b0e0-e427-4f5e-a584-bb5eda19fb66-000000/_Q96DfVyg_-4arPoEoQNd5vgxvHIFl9fPFdgBZGbW90=434" target="_blank"&gt;FluBites&lt;/a&gt; report. Information on flu activity can also be found on&nbsp;&lt;a href="https://links-2.govdelivery.com/CL0/https:%2F%2Fpublic.tableau.com%2Fapp%2Fprofile%2Foregon.public.health.division.acute.and.communicable.disease.pre%2Fviz%2FOregonsRespiratoryVirusData%2FTableofContents%3Futm_medium=email%26utm_source=govdelivery/1/0101019ae6cc9a96-bbf9b0e0-e427-4f5e-a584-bb5eda19fb66-000000/5aMid9Ziwmc_imBBYjWQ-D3D_NsfoKh6yHY26eCi81A=434" target="_blank"&gt;Oregon&rsquo;s Respiratory Virus Data dashboard&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flu is a contagious, respiratory tract infection caused by the influenza virus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Symptoms can include fever, cough, sore throat, stuffy and runny nose, muscle aches, headache and fatigue. It can result in mild to severe illness, including hospitalization and death.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) and the American Academy of Family Physicians (AAFP) recommend seasonal flu vaccination for everyone ages 6 months or older.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nearly 1 million people in Oregon have been vaccinated so far this year, which is about 5% lower than at the same time last year. The flu vaccination rate has been in decline for several years.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Certain groups, including older adults, young children, pregnant people and people with certain health conditions, are at higher risk for serious complications from influenza.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hospitalization rate is highest for people ages 65 and older, but flu seasons are unpredictable and even young and healthy people can develop severe disease.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best way people can protect themselves and others is by getting a flu vaccine every year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People can get the COVID-19, influenza and RSV vaccines by contacting their health plan, health care provider, county public health clinic or federally qualified health center. Parents and caregivers of children can also search for a clinic by calling 211 or visiting &lt;a href="https://links-2.govdelivery.com/CL0/https:%2F%2Fwww.211info.org%2F%3Futm_medium=email%26utm_source=govdelivery/1/0101019ae6cc9a96-bbf9b0e0-e427-4f5e-a584-bb5eda19fb66-000000/pO3LheL-rN9a4sNxvIe8cHp5uwSD8wsvUzoYoSb3X5g=434" target="_blank"&gt;211info.org.&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This &lt;a href="https://links-2.govdelivery.com/CL0/https:%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fwatch%3Futm_medium=email%26utm_source=govdelivery%26v=tPWtQWb_0YI/1/0101019ae6cc9a96-bbf9b0e0-e427-4f5e-a584-bb5eda19fb66-000000/gqKKm95XZd8IAlogKcvXe244HyagwvItmLQWq8Ph8-U=434" target="_blank"&gt;video&lt;/a&gt; reminds people not to let influenza take us out of the picture this year.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

